abstract: |
  Spec-driven development (SDD) tools let coding agents work from written specifications instead of chat instructions. We ask how two such tools, BMAD and GitHub Spec Kit, support the step from requirements to implementation and the handling of a requirement change. Using a comparative case study, two team members built the same Java Swing calculator, one with each tool, from one frozen set of requirements (8 functional and 5 non-functional requirements, 19 acceptance criteria). The same change request, which adds square, square root and reciprocal operations (11 requirements, 22 criteria), was then given to both. Independent evaluators later ran common execution sheets. Both workflows reported all 22 change criteria and all 19 baseline re-checks as passed. The independent baseline evaluation gave BMAD 19 passes and Spec Kit 18 passes and one blocked check (an offline-launch prerequisite), which is a difference in evidence, not in demonstrated correctness. Spec Kit's independent change evaluation left more inspectable raw evidence. Effort, change-impact recall, native traceability and rubric ratings were not measured, so we make no claim about an overall winner. The study has one case, one run per workflow and confounded operator, agent and environment.
---

# Introduction

Coding agents now produce working software from short instructions, but the intent behind that output is easily lost when the instructions live only in a chat [@agarwal2026]. Spec-driven development (SDD) responds by making the specification an operational input: a person writes or approves a specification, a plan and a task list, and the agent generates code against them [@piskala2026; @claudino2026]. Tools such as GitHub Spec Kit [@speckit] and BMAD [@bmad] package this idea into installable workflows.

From a requirements engineering (RE) point of view, the interesting question is not which tool generates better code. It is how each workflow carries requirements through specification, design, tasks, code and tests, whether those links can be followed, what happens when a requirement changes, and how much of the original requirement set the result satisfies. The sources we read describe SDD practices and test claims about them at repository scale [@claudino2026; @agarwal2026; @hill2026], but they do not compare two named SDD tools on one controlled software case with a common change request.

This report presents such a comparison as a small, bounded case study. Two members of our team each operated one tool to build the same desktop calculator from the same frozen requirements, then applied the same change request. Two other members evaluated the results independently with shared checklists. The study is descriptive: the unit of comparison is the whole tool, agent and operator workflow, and we do not claim to isolate the effect of either tool.

We make three contributions. First, we describe both tools as they were installed and used, with their native artefacts and change paths. Second, we report acceptance outcomes, change-impact observations and process observations for the baseline and the change, and we state for every number which evidence layer it comes from. Third, we list which planned measures could not be calculated and why. The report first gives the background and the method, which includes the research questions. It then describes the tools, presents the results, discusses the questions and the threats to validity, and ends with a conclusion and the AI-use declaration.

# Background

## Spec-Driven Development

SDD treats the specification as the source of truth and the code as a generated or verified secondary product. Piskala distinguishes three levels of specification rigour: spec-first, where a specification is written before the code; spec-anchored, where it is kept alongside the code; and spec-as-source, where the specification is the main artefact [@piskala2026]. We use this vocabulary only to describe the workflows. We do not rank the tools by it; the abstract does not present it as a measure for comparing tools.

Claudino et al. scored specification, plan and task artefacts of 114 open-source SDD repositories against ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148 and derived a framework of 40 practices in 10 quality dimensions [@claudino2026]. Agarwal et al. combined a thematic analysis, 21 practitioner interviews and a corpus of 470,795 specification files from 266 repositories; they report that implementation detail in specifications may help agents but makes specifications more likely to go stale, and that specification and code are usually checked against each other only when a change is finished [@agarwal2026]. Hill analysed 88,052 pull requests in 119 repositories and found no meaningful difference in defect introduction between specified and unspecified pull requests after matching; the specification artefacts in that sample were mostly references to issues and tickets, and the author notes that the effect of adopting SDD tools remains untested [@hill2026]. We read only the abstract pages of these three works, and two of them are preprints whose peer-review status we have not established. They justify caution: a specification is not automatically a quality improvement.

Vibe coding, meaning prompting an agent without a written specification, is named in our project brief as a contrasting approach. It is not an experimental condition here.

## Requirements Engineering Concepts Used

We use four RE concepts as operational definitions. *Traceability* is the ability to follow a requirement forward to design, tasks, code and tests and back. We separate links created by the workflow itself (native links) from links reconstructed afterwards by an evaluator, because the second kind says little about what the workflow supports. *Change-impact analysis* is the statement, made before coding, of which requirements, design elements, code and tests a change will touch. *Acceptance criteria* are checks that turn a requirement into something an evaluator can observe. *Non-functional requirements* (NFRs) are quality constraints such as launch behaviour or the absence of network access; in a small GUI application they are the hardest to turn into observable criteria.

## Related Work

We did not conduct a systematic literature search; the sources above were found through general web searches and the reference lists of other papers. Within them, the work closest to ours analyses repositories at scale [@claudino2026; @agarwal2026; @hill2026]. Our study differs in design: it is small, runs two named tools on one shared case, and applies one requirement change to both. Its methodological basis is guidance for software engineering case studies [@runeson2009] and for measurement and data collection in empirical studies [@kitchenham2002]. Measures are organised with the Goal Question Metric approach [@basili1994]. We read the bibliographic records of these three sources, not their full texts.

# Method

## Research Questions

Our main question is: *How do SDD tools support the transition from requirements to implementation, and how well does the result match the original requirements?* It is split into four questions.

- **RQ1.** How does each tool create and structure specifications, including formats, levels of detail, templates and the role of the human?
- **RQ2.** How are requirements turned into design, tasks, code and tests, and is the link between these artefacts traceable?
- **RQ3.** How does each tool handle changes to the specification, and does the specification remain aligned with the code?
- **RQ4.** How closely does the generated implementation satisfy the original requirements?

The team's later analysis used a different set of five questions that ask which workflow performed better, handled the change better and produced better documentation. We map its findings onto RQ1 to RQ4 and do not adopt those questions as headings.

## Case and Change Request

The case is a desktop calculator in Java Swing with two operand fields, four operation buttons, a Calculate and a Clear button, and inline error messages. Arithmetic is exact in decimals; division rounds half-up to ten places; results are shown as plain decimal text. The error messages are fixed, and the error priority is empty input, then no operation, then an invalid number, then an arithmetic error such as division by zero. The baseline requirements were frozen on 2026-10-23.

The change request, CR-01, adds three unary operations: square, square root and reciprocal. The First number field is used and the Second number is ignored. The square is exact; square root and reciprocal are rounded half-up to ten places; a negative square root and a reciprocal of zero are errors; and the criteria require all baseline checks to be re-run. [Table](#tbl-case) gives the size of the case.

| Phase | Requirement IDs | Parent acceptance criteria | Re-checked baseline parents |
|---|---|---|---|
| Baseline | 8 functional + 5 non-functional | 19 (BASE-AC-01..19) | n/a |
| CR-01 | 11 functional (CR-FR-01..11) | 22 (CR-AC-01..22) | 19 |

Table: Size of the shared case per phase. {#tbl-case}

<!-- source: data/case-counts.csv columns=Phase,Requirement IDs,Parent acceptance criteria,Re-checked baseline parents rows=all sha256=d54e5edaa44b7cb9 -->

The case has known limits. Four baseline NFRs have no directly linked acceptance criterion and three criteria have no requirement ID. This is a limitation of the shared input and applies to both tracks.

## Experimental Procedure

[Figure](#fig-study) shows the design. Each track started from the same frozen requirements and acceptance criteria and kept its tool's native workflow. The fairness rules were: the same inputs for both, no sharing of generated artefacts between tracks, native workflows left intact, human interventions recorded and every deviation documented. Shared ambiguities and decisions were recorded in a common experiment log, which both tracks had to follow. Each track froze its baseline with a git tag before CR-01 was released. For CR-01, each track produced a pre-coding impact analysis, implemented the change and froze the result with a second tag. The shared baseline snapshot was `baseline-frozen`; the BMAD change snapshot is the commit tagged `bmad-cr01-frozen`.

![Study design: one frozen baseline and one change request (CR-01) given to both tracks, followed by independent evaluation.](figures/study-workflow.pdf){#fig-study}

Two members who had built neither calculator evaluated the results with identical execution sheets for the baseline criteria and the change criteria. One evaluated the BMAD track and one evaluated the Spec Kit track, the latter with assistance from an AI coding assistant. For Spec Kit, the evaluation used the baseline snapshot designated by the study owner, because the track's own operator results had been recorded at an earlier commit and the track had not declared a freeze; the log records that differences between the two commits may exist. All sources for this report, including both tracks' evidence and the experiment log, are in the team repository.

## Evaluation Approach and Measures

We report acceptance outcomes as Pass, Fail, Blocked or Not run. A criterion counts as Pass only if the behaviour was observed. A Blocked criterion is not a Fail, a Not run criterion is not a zero, and a later manual completion does not replace the earlier Blocked record. Three layers of evidence are kept apart: operator reports, independent evaluator observations, and native automated tests. Counts of native tests are context only, because the two tracks use different test designs, layers and environments. Following Runeson and Höst, we describe validity threats by category rather than hiding them in an overall score [@runeson2009], and following Kitchenham et al. we state counting rules explicitly [@kitchenham2002].

The measures come from a catalogue of sixteen measures (M01 to M16) that the team derived from five evaluation goals with the Goal Question Metric approach [@basili1994]. Those that matter here are acceptance outcomes (M01), native and reconstructed traceability (M03, M13), defects (M04), regressions after the change (M06), change-impact recall (M07), interventions and rework (M08, M12), effort and duration (M09, M10), rubric ratings (M11) and native test inventory (M16). [Table](#tbl-metrics) in the Results states which could be calculated.

