## BMAD

BMAD, expanded by its maintainers as the Breakthrough Method for Agile AI-Driven Development, is an open-source framework that covers deciding what to build, how the parts fit and how the work changes as one learns [@bmad]. Its documentation describes a delivery loop of clarifying a vague idea, planning a large idea, building and verifying a small change, and learning and adjusting. It offers specialised perspectives for product, architecture, UX, development and testing. The installed native flow in our track ran from an optional product brief to a PRD, an optional UX step, an architecture, epics and stories, sprint planning, build, and optional code review and QA.

[Figure](#fig-bmad) shows the path actually followed. The operator used a fast path for the PRD and architecture, skipped the UX step, and had the agent judge the readiness gate. The PRD holds ten functional and five non-functional requirements. The architecture spine records ten architecture decisions. Work was split into three epics and nine stories, each built with `bmad-build` and checked by three review layers, except two stories where the operator chose a minimal path. For the change request, `bmad-correct-course` produced a sprint change proposal and an impact analysis before any code; after the owner approved a new Epic 4 with four stories, the PRD, architecture and epics were amended and the stories were built.

![BMAD as used in the BMAD track (version 6.12.4): workflow steps, the artefact each step leaves, and the change path.](figures/bmad-workflow.pdf){#fig-bmad}

## GitHub Spec Kit

GitHub Spec Kit is an open-source toolkit that gives AI coding agents structured processes, reusable templates and documented outcomes [@speckit]. Its core SDD process runs in the agent's chat: `/speckit-constitution` records project principles once, `/speckit-specify` writes the specification, `/speckit-plan` turns it into a technical approach, `/speckit-tasks` derives tasks, and `/speckit-implement` produces code; a convergence command can repeat until the result matches the plan. The installed version also provides skills for clarification, checklists and a read-only consistency analysis.

[Figure](#fig-speckit) shows the path actually followed. The constitution was drafted from the shared documents and amended once for the change request (version 1.0.0 to 1.1.0) with the owner's approval. `/speckit-specify` raised three clarification questions, which the owner answered. The plan produced a research file, a data model, contracts and a quickstart; the owner approved the plan before `/speckit-tasks` generated 34 baseline tasks. `/speckit-analyze` reported 11 findings (none critical, two high, two medium, seven low) before any code, and fixes were applied after the owner's approval. For CR-01 the track wrote an impact analysis first and then ran the same chain in a new feature folder, with 49 tasks and a second analysis of 10 findings.

![GitHub Spec Kit as used in the Spec Kit track (specify-cli 1.2.3): commands, the artefact each leaves, and the change path.](figures/speckit-workflow.pdf){#fig-speckit}

## Capability Comparison

[Table](#tbl-cap) compares what the two workflows offered, on the criteria of the project brief. Entries describe what the artefacts and logs show; they are not ratings.

| Criterion | BMAD | Spec Kit |
|---|---|---|
| Specification structure | PRD, architecture decisions, epics, story specs | Constitution, spec, plan, research, data model, contracts, tasks |
| Requirement-to-task link | Epics and stories; requirement-to-test table in the evidence | Requirement IDs tagged in the spec; "Requirement Coverage" table added to the plan (not native) |
| Ambiguity handling | Agent questions at start; review layers; gaps raised in the log | Clarification questions in `/speckit-specify`; `/speckit-analyze` before code |
| Acceptance criteria | Shared criteria; a missing NFR criterion fixed at the readiness gate | All 19 criteria mapped to tasks |
| Test generation | Tests per story; review layers added test patches | Tests as tasks; red runs recorded before code |
| NFRs | NFR-1 to NFR-5 in the PRD | Baseline NFR IDs tagged in the spec |
| Change handling | `bmad-correct-course`, change proposal, new epic, amended PRD and architecture | Impact analysis, new feature folder, amended constitution, new spec, plan and tasks |
| Human control | Owner approvals; standing approval per story | Owner approvals recorded as AP-01 to AP-06; gates before tasks and code |

Table: Capabilities of the two workflows as observed in the tracks. {#tbl-cap}

# Results

All counts in this section are as recorded in the tracks' evidence files and in the team's consolidated results; we did not re-run any test or launch either application. [Figure](#fig-acc) summarises the acceptance outcomes.

![Parent acceptance criteria passed. Base (op.): baseline, operator report, of 19. Base (ind.): baseline, independent evaluator, of 19 (Spec Kit also had one Blocked). CR-01: of 22. Regress.: baseline re-check after CR-01, of 19.](figures/acceptance-outcomes.pdf){#fig-acc}

## Specification Structure and Traceability

Both workflows produced structured, linked artefacts before coding (RQ1, RQ2). BMAD's chain runs from the PRD and the architecture decisions to epics, stories, tests and a requirement-to-test table. Spec Kit's runs from the constitution and the specification to the plan, tasks, tests and a results file with a traceability table. Both contain candidate links from requirement to code and tests. A verified count of native links, which would give a traceability measure (M03), has not been established for either track, and links rebuilt afterwards by an evaluator (M13) have to be kept apart from it. We therefore do not report a traceability percentage.

The shared requirements were not complete enough to be implemented as written. Both operators had to resolve ambiguities through the common log: what happens when no operation is selected, how ties are rounded for negative numbers, whether `.5` and `5.` are valid, and when a shown result is cleared. Both tracks found gaps independently. The change request also had gaps that surfaced when the BMAD track read it, among them whether the Second number field stays visible and how an empty input is reported. Gaps were therefore found in both phases, by operating the workflows, not by a review of the requirements.

## Baseline Implementation

Both operators reported all 19 baseline criteria as passed. The independent evaluation differs (RQ4). The BMAD evaluator, who recorded results on a reported clean checkout, reported 19 passes, but the offline-launch criterion has no evidence that networking was disabled and criterion-level screenshots and logs were not attached. The Spec Kit evaluator recorded 18 passes, no failures and one Blocked criterion: the offline launch was not executed because the network prerequisite could not be established. That Blocked criterion is not a product failure, and the difference between 19 and 18 reflects recorded coverage and evidence provenance, not demonstrated correctness. The Spec Kit evaluation retained real-window automation output, screenshots and observation logs.

The reported examples cover the four operations, decimal precision and rounding, negative inputs, division by zero, invalid and missing operands, Clear, recovery after an error, sequential calculations and the Enter key. They do not show exhaustive correctness or that every NFR is satisfied.

## Change Request CR-01

Both tracks reported all 22 change criteria as passed and all 19 baseline criteria as passed again after the change, with no regression in the checked set (RQ3, RQ4). The two tracks' evidence is not equally strong. The Spec Kit evaluation retained a frozen-snapshot build output, GUI observations and screenshots, and its offline check was first Blocked and later marked Pass through a human-reported follow-up; both records stay visible. The BMAD evaluator's change results are recorded in the evaluator sheets, but the exact frozen checkout and criterion-level raw observations were not established to the same standard. The final 22 of 22 and 19 of 19 are combined reported statuses, not 22 independently reproduced evidence bundles.

The 22 change criteria test new behaviour and the 19 re-checks test preserved behaviour, so the numbers do not form a series that grows from 19 to 22. As context only, the cited native test suites grew from 172 to 316 tests for BMAD (with 80 window tests skipped in a headless run and none failing on a display) and from 81 to 116 for Spec Kit with no failures; the two suites differ in design and cannot be compared as quality.

[Table](#tbl-change) shows change handling by dimension. Both impact analyses were useful but incomplete. The Spec Kit forecast named five source files that were expected to change and five that were expected to stay unchanged, and that classification held; later, work beyond the forecast appeared: a constitution update, a changed `OperationTest.java` and an extra helper for error-area size. The BMAD track met GUI operation-button tests and an initial-state helper that its analysis had not listed. A product defect, a wrong test input or expectation and a documentation edit are different units, so counting them against each other would mislead.

| Dimension | BMAD | Spec Kit |
|---|---|---|
| Change planning | Story- and architecture-driven impact analysis; review layers | Impact forecast, then spec, plan and tasks |
| Unplanned effect | GUI operation-button tests; initial-state helper | Constitution update; `OperationTest.java`; error-area helper |
| Product defect found | None established in the case descriptions | Negative square-root message clipped in the GUI; found by visual review, then fixed |
| Test or documentation correction | Test input `1e41` against `1e40`; test-only review patches; one Javadoc correction | One wrong test expectation corrected |
| Impact recall (M07) | Not calculable | Not calculable |
| Rework (M12) | Candidate episodes only | Candidate episodes only |

Table: Change handling in CR-01 by dimension. {#tbl-change}

## Process Observations

Several observations come from the process records of single runs and carry no statistical weight. In the BMAD track, the review layers returned many items per story, and about half of them were verified as false or rejected as low priority, so every item had to be triaged. The story split by architecture layer made Epic 2 mostly a verification exercise instead of new work. In the Spec Kit track, `/speckit-analyze` found coverage and consistency gaps before any code, and they were fixed after approval; the one product defect of the change phase was found by looking at the real window, not by an automated test.

Both tracks recorded deviations from their tool's native process, listed in the evidence files. The BMAD track used a fast path, skipped the UX step, did not run the PRD and architecture reviewer subagents, had the agent judge the readiness gate, and replaced per-story approval by a standing approval. The Spec Kit track did not run a template-resolve step for two skills, added a "Requirement Coverage" table to the plan that the tool does not produce, and left one gap, the square root of negative zero, unspecified by the owner's decision. Both tracks also had documentation drift: a BMAD status file still described the change as uncommitted after it had been committed, and a Spec Kit plan referred to a log entry before it existed.

[Table](#tbl-metrics) states which measures could be calculated. Most of them could not.

| Measure | Construct | Status in this study |
|---|---|---|
| M01 | Parent acceptance outcomes | Calculable as pass ratios per phase; subcase-level evidence incomplete |
| M02 | Requirement-level status | Partial; no verified numeric status |
| M03 / M13 | Native / evaluator-reconstructed traceability | Not scored; semantic link verification pending |
| M04 / M05 | Product defects / late-defect share | Descriptive examples only; no complete census |
| M06 | Baseline regressions after CR-01 | Zero reported; observation provenance unequal |
| M07 | Change-impact omission and recall | Not calculable; no adjudicated reference set |
| M08 / M12 | Human interventions / rework episodes | Illustrative only; no comparable census |
| M09 / M10 | Active effort / elapsed duration | Not calculable; no measured sessions |
| M11 / M14 | Rubric ratings J1-J7 / reviewer agreement | Not rated; no composite score |
| M15 | Evidence completeness | Not calculable as a percentage |
| M16 | Native test inventory | Context only; no cross-track quality inference |

Table: Disposition of the catalogue measures in this study. {#tbl-metrics}

