# Discussion

## Answers to the Research Questions

**RQ1 (specification structure).** The tools structure specifications differently. BMAD builds a layered set of planning documents (PRD, architecture decisions, epics, stories) around what the system should do and why it is designed as it is. Spec Kit builds a chain of principles, specification, plan and tasks around what is specified and which tasks follow. In both, the human approved plans and answered questions, and in both the operators had to settle ambiguities in the shared requirements through the log. We cannot say which structure is better; no rubric ratings of specification clarity exist.

**RQ2 (traceability).** Both produced artefacts from which a requirement can plausibly be followed into tasks, code and tests, and each track kept a requirement-to-test table. We could not verify native link coverage, so the answer is limited to saying that both made links available and that Spec Kit's coverage table was an addition to its plan, not a tool output.

**RQ3 (change handling and alignment).** Both tracks produced a pre-coding impact analysis, updated their planning artefacts, implemented CR-01 and reported no regression. BMAD's path went through a change proposal and an amended PRD, architecture and epics; Spec Kit's through a new feature folder and an amended constitution. Neither impact analysis was complete, and an adjudicated reference set for impact recall does not exist, so we cannot rank them. Whether specification and code stayed aligned was checked in both tracks only at the end of the change, in line with the late spec-code checking that practitioners report [@agarwal2026]. That observation is about our two runs; it is not a finding about the tools.

**RQ4 (match to requirements).** Both implementations reported full success on the shared criteria, and no independent run observed a product failure. The independent baseline difference of one Blocked criterion is a difference in evidence. Four NFRs have no linked acceptance criterion, so the evaluation says little about them. Passing 19 of 19 or 22 of 22 parent criteria does not establish exhaustive correctness.

Taken together, the answers are dimension-specific. [Table](#tbl-strength) lists what the evidence supports and where it stops. We make no claim about which workflow performed better overall, handled the change better or produced better documentation, because effort, impact recall, rework, native traceability and rubric ratings were not measured, and observation layers differ between the tracks.

| Workflow | Supported strengths | Constraints and evidence limits |
|---|---|---|
| BMAD | Coherent PRD, architecture, epic and story organisation; review-oriented change handling; explicit impact analysis | Unplanned GUI test and helper work; test-input and documentation corrections; independent change evidence less complete |
| Spec Kit | Constitution, spec, plan and task chain; impact forecast with changed and unchanged files; retained independent build and GUI evidence; defect found and fixed | Unplanned constitution, test and helper changes; a GUI defect before correction; offline check first Blocked |

Table: Strengths and constraints per workflow, with evidence limits. {#tbl-strength}

## Implications

Four practical points follow from the study, for this case and not for all projects. A change should be judged against new behaviour and preserved behaviour together, because the two sets of criteria answer different questions. Planning and impact documents should be followed by a check in the real window, because the clipped error message passed automated tests. Keeping the original Blocked attempt next to a later manual completion avoids hindsight bias. Finally, an independent evaluation needs a frozen commit, the commands, timestamps and the relevant environment state, and a measure such as impact recall needs a prediction fixed before coding and a reference set agreed afterwards. Effort, rework and rubric data would have to be collected while the work is done; they cannot be reconstructed from commit times.

## Threats to Validity

*Construct validity.* A parent Pass does not prove that every subcase was independently executed, and native test counts are not acceptance coverage. We keep these layers separate in every table.

*Internal validity.* The tool, the agent and the operator are confounded: one operator per tool, two operating systems, and a model change at the start of the Spec Kit track. The study owner operated the BMAD track and wrote the change request, which may bias the BMAD track toward familiarity with the shared documents. The comparison is descriptive and not a randomised benchmark.

*Reliability.* Each workflow was run once, so we have no estimate of the run-to-run variability of the agent. The BMAD change-phase evaluation lacks an archived frozen checkout and raw observations of the same depth as the Spec Kit package. The Spec Kit baseline evaluation used a snapshot designated by the owner, not declared by the operator. Neither track saved every prompt verbatim.

*Conclusion validity.* Missing measures are not treated as zero, and no composite score is computed. One small application, one change request and two workflows do not support claims about SDD tools in general.

*Shared input.* The ambiguities in the baseline and in CR-01, the four NFRs without acceptance criteria and the three criteria without requirement IDs affect both tracks equally, but they limit what the evaluation can show.

# Conclusion

Within the limits of one case, both workflows carried the same requirements through specification, planning, tasks, code and tests, handled the same change request with a pre-coding impact analysis, and reported all 22 change criteria and all 19 baseline re-checks as passed. The independent baseline results, 19 passes for BMAD and 18 passes with one Blocked for Spec Kit, differ in evidence provenance and not in demonstrated correctness. Spec Kit's independent change evaluation was more inspectable, and BMAD's planning documents gave a continuous account of architecture and story decisions. Both required human decisions to settle ambiguities in the shared requirements, and both met effects of the change that their impact analyses did not list. We found no basis for an overall ranking. Future work should fix an impact reference set before coding, measure effort and rework prospectively, collect independent ratings, repeat the runs, and verify native traceability link by link.

# AI-Use Declaration

AI tools were used in four ways. (1) Both calculators were built with Claude Code and the model `claude-sonnet-5-5`, which acted as the operator's coding agent in each track; the Spec Kit track started on `claude-opus-5-5` and switched to `claude-sonnet-5-5`. The agent produced the planning artefacts, code, tests and evidence drafts through the BMAD 6.12.4 skills (including review subagents) and the Spec Kit 1.2.3 skills; the operators approved plans and recorded decisions. (2) The Spec Kit baseline and change evaluations were run with the assistance of an AI coding assistant. (3) The literature registers were produced with AI-assisted source discovery and drafting, and an evaluation skill (`evaluate-sdd-workflows`) was used to prepare evaluations. (4) This report was drafted with Claude Code and a document-production workflow, from the team's repository files and from bibliographic records and abstract pages fetched on 2026-11-06; every reference was checked against such a record, and no full text of the cited works was read. [[EVIDENCE NEEDED: confirmation by the authors that the text of this report was reviewed and approved by a human author, and the exact wording required by the course for the AI-use declaration]]

# References
